.Martin. wrote:
> Another set of questions:
> 
> 1. What am I supposed to do with a surplus of weapon/armour? Shall I
> just drop some daggers that I don't use?
I recommend you keep about 6-10 daggers for throwing at enemies before
they're close enough to hit in melee. If you have more, stash them
somewhere (like one of those large boxes).  Other surplus gear can be
sold in shops when you find one.  Generally you want to stay
unburdened.  If you become burdened, put some of the heavier and less
immediately useful gear in your stash (unidentified potions are a good
thing to stash until you have scrolls of identify).

As far as armor goes, if it's listed as blessed or uncursed, try it on
and see if your armor class (AC) goes down (lower is better).  It's
not unusual to find some heavily enchanted armor early on if you try
everything on.  There are a number of ways to determine if an item is
blessed or cursed, so let us know if you want spoilers (and to what
extent).

.Martin. wrote:
> 2. How do you search for hidden doors ('s')? I've read that constantly
> tapping on the 's' key is discouraged from. Someone recommended
> eg. 'n10s' which will search 10x. Now my question is wether it'll search
> ten times in the square I'm standing now or will it search as I move on
> to some other places. In other words, is that command placed in one unit
> of time /one turn or it uses up 10 turns / units of time? I hope you'll
> understand what I mean.
> thank you
It'll search 10 times in the square you're standing on, but it still
takes 10 turns to do it.  It's exactly the same as pressing 's' ten
times, just more convenient.  And the the 'n (number) (command)' thing
works for any command, not just search.  The reason it's recommended
is because if you're repeatedly tapping on the 's' key when a monster
comes by, it might get in a few hits before you notice and stop
hitting 's'.  The 'n (number) (command)' approach will interrupt
itself in a lot of circumstances where you might want to stop
repeating the action. (I don't know the full list of things that
can make it stop; perhaps someone else here does.)  You should also
look at the 'g' (go) command, which is performs a similar function
for movement.


